PARS = [
("The third limit is answered", """The third limit is answered by the separation of two cores. A simulation core owns the hazard state, and a decision core reads it through confidence-rated observations. Hence, each core is validated on its own. The simulation core is validated by blind hindcast against satellite records of documented fires. The decision core is measured by paired comparison on that validated core, so the benefit of opening the decision space is read as a difference on identical worlds. The decision core is distributed over regional agents, decides in a small fraction of a second, and its cost grows slowly with the number of agents, so it suits real-time operation."""),

("Surface fire spread is described", """Surface fire spread is described by Rothermel-type rate-of-spread models driven by fuel, moisture, wind, and slope {7;27;28}. These models are implemented in operational tools such as FlamMap {8} and in cellular simulators such as Cell2Fire {29}. Decision support built on these models ranges from strategic risk assessment {4;9} and multi-criteria allocation {10;16;30} to the integrated WFDSS platform {11}, whose field use has been studied extensively {12;13;14}. In all of them the decision logic is authored offline and the intervention menu is fixed. Fire management economics frames the decision as cost plus loss {3;31}. This framing is the basis of the cost used here."""),

("Decision analysis has long assumed", """Decision analysis has long assumed that the set of alternatives is given before evaluation begins {32;33}. Arbel and Tong {18} identified option generation as a neglected phase that precedes choice. Colorni and Tsoukias {19;34} formalized the construction of the alternative set as a recursion of decision problems. Hofstede {20} observed that planning systems which hard-wire the open aspects of a problem are not adopted. Cognitive evidence points the same way. Option generation in ill-structured problems depends on ideation beyond memory retrieval, and generating more options raises the quality of the best option while it lowers the mean quality of the set {35}. Operators build courses of action from recognized patterns rather than from enumerated menus {23}. Hence, a DSS should generate alternatives during operation, and the generated set must be filtered by a strict, external evaluation."""),

("Three families of mechanisms", """Three families of mechanisms open a decision space. The first searches a fixed parameter space for structurally different solutions, as in the generator-evaluator separation of active decision support {22} and modeling to generate alternatives {21}. The second composes alternatives from modules under compatibility constraints {17;36}. The third extends the action model at run time, as in open-world planning and action discovery {37;38}. In all three the opened space stays bounded by a basis the system already understands. Hence, the open space is an open composition over a closed and verified basis, and this is the sense used here. Two design consequences follow. Generation must be cheap to reject, because most of the composition space leaves the outcome unchanged, and the evaluator must be independent of the generator. Evolving Takagi-Sugeno (TS) models create and tune rules online within the existing vocabulary {39;40}. Value-based selectors learn when to change structure {41}. Generative models draft rules outside the current structure {42}. VODA places the first inside the closed layer and the last inside the open layer, behind one satisficing acceptance rule {43}."""),

("Recent wildfire and disaster systems", """Recent wildfire and disaster systems have added learned and generative components. However, the intervention space has stayed closed or unchecked. Dubey and Dubey {44} couple a Transformer forecaster with fifteen expert rules that map predicted risk to four fixed response levels. Chandra et al. {45} draw suggested actions from a predefined ontology and check them for consistency only. Cheng et al. {46} let LLMs weight a fixed hierarchy of indicators. Gerolimos et al. {47} call for open-world learning in disaster management, but the openness concerns the recognition of unseen event classes while the response stays a mapping to familiar actions. At the other end, multi-agent LLMs produce free-text response plans whose hallucinations are contained by retrieval grounding, output schemas, or post-hoc grading {48;49}. Cui et al. {50} conclude that external verification through physics-based simulation is required. Nevertheless, they confine their own agent to a fixed tool library. WildfireKR {51} is the closest neighbour. It is an LLM agent that automates calibrated spread simulation, but it produces suppression-free envelopes and leaves intervention design to the analyst. The remaining gap is a system whose intervention space is open at run time, in which every new intervention is built from effects a physical model can execute and admission is decided by that model rather than by the proposer, a schema, or the decision maker alone."""),

("Operational assessments arrive", """Operational assessments arrive as graded, linguistic judgments, whose formal counterpart is partial membership {52}. Domain knowledge enters as rules of the canonical conjunctive form. Zero-order TS inference turns rule activations into a crisp intensity as a firing-strength weighted average of constant consequents {39}. The choice is architectural. Candidates are judged inside shadow forecasts that run many times per incident, so centroid integration would be too costly. Moreover, the outputs are intensity commands, and adaptation needs numeric consequents with predictable effect while antecedents stay linguistic. Applying inference directly over a rich observation vector is combinatorially infeasible, since ten features with five terms each span nearly ten million antecedent combinations. A conceptual middle layer reduces this to five decision concepts. Each concept answers exactly once one of the questions operators ask {3;23}. Thus, the antecedent space shrinks to 3125 combinations, only the active subset is instantiated, and the concepts double as the vocabulary in which every recommendation is explained."""),
]
